WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.200
FIPS 204 is the Module-Lattice-Based Digital Signature Standard.

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:07.463
The standardized algorithm name is ML-DSA, associated with the Dilithium family.

00:00:07.463 --> 00:00:11.725
Digital signatures provide authentication, integrity, and non-repudiation at a practical level.

00:00:11.725 --> 00:00:15.213
They help prove that a message, artifact, certificate, update,

00:00:15.213 --> 00:00:19.863
or transaction was signed by the holder of a private key and

00:00:19.863 --> 00:00:21.800
was not changed after signing.

00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:26.450
Signature migration can be operationally harder than key establishment migration because signatures

00:00:26.450 --> 00:00:28.388
appear in long-lived trust structures.

00:00:28.388 --> 00:00:31.875
Certificates, code signing, firmware signing, document signing, package repositories,

00:00:31.875 --> 00:00:33.813
identity systems, secure boot chains,

00:00:33.813 --> 00:00:38.463
and update pipelines may all depend on signature formats and verification behavior.

00:00:38.463 --> 00:00:41.950
Some signed artifacts need to remain verifiable for years.

00:00:41.950 --> 00:00:45.050
ML-DSA gives organizations a standardized post-quantum signature option,

00:00:45.050 --> 00:00:47.375
but adoption still requires engineering work.

00:00:47.375 --> 00:00:51.250
Certificate formats, protocol support, hardware security modules, key management systems,

00:00:51.250 --> 00:00:53.575
build pipelines, verification libraries, device firmware,

00:00:53.575 --> 00:00:56.288
and policy controls may all need updates.

00:00:56.288 --> 00:01:02.100
Teams should avoid assuming that a new algorithm name automatically creates an end-to-end migration path.

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:06.750
The practical migration question is where trust depends on signatures and how

00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:08.688
long that trust must last.

00:01:08.688 --> 00:01:13.338
A short-lived internal token has a different risk profile from firmware that

00:01:13.338 --> 00:01:16.050
may be installed on devices for years.

00:01:16.050 --> 00:01:20.700
A code signing key has different operational requirements from a service certificate.

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:26.900
Understanding those differences helps teams prioritize signature migration instead of treating every use case the same.
